no one  is right it's really weird to sit there  and listen to all of that  those of you who know me or being in my  classes  i know that i do love to talk  but when i sat down and thinking about  what i was going to say today  i was truly at a loss i had no idea  where to start what to say  what words could i say that expressed  the gratitude  that i have for this place what kind of  speech  could adequately convey the impact  that these people and this community had  on me  nothing i could ever say would come  close but i have to try  so that's why i'm interested so  what do i want to tell you i watch truth  do i love to leave  green in your ears as i say goodbye  and i think what i want to do the most  key way to end  is to tell you a story this is a story  about paying things forward painful  one of the funniest things i experienced  while i was here as a teacher is that so  many of you  look to me as kind of like a version of  you but older and  there is a sense in which that is true  but i think the reality is that  i am one of the most unbruce-ish  kinds of people that this school has  ever produced so let's try and  justify that statement some of you  noticed in years 11 and 12  i studied no sciences uh this is  sorry the reason why i studied those  sciences is not because i disliked  science  but because i wanted so much to study  the nine units of humanities that i took  english  history drama i loved them to pieces  and i still do now i always kind of had  lingering in the back of my mind but  that was  a little bit weird for a jeju student  but i have very powerful reminder that  last year actually when year 12 i was  supervising  your first hsc chemistry task i was  walking up and down  the tables from this hall and i looked  around and i thought  this feels like the entire grade all  here  sitting the chemistry paper it must only  be the widows who don't study chemistry  oh that's me and so  i came to that realization but why do i  study such an unusual set of subjects it  wasn't just to be a sort of hsc to start  like physics is so mainstream  that was not the reason rather i studied  those subjects because  my teachers had encouraged me to pursue  those kinds of things because they saw  probably better than i did but they were  really good fit for my personality and  my character  and my interests it was the same when i  decided  i realized that i wanted to study  education it was weird  and unusual for a jj student but it  didn't matter  because james bruce was a place that  embraced the pursuit of excellence  whatever form it took and it still is  that kind of place  now when i was in university i went and  got my degree in education  but i never expected to actually come  back here  and teach so when i was offered a  temporary position here by mr wayne who  used to be the deputy principal  i literally jumped at the offer  now why am i so excited about working  here  it wasn't because this was a great place  to work even though  it is it wasn't because i lived  so close to the school even though i did  and it wasn't because the students were  so smart  even though you are i've used coming  back  as an opportunity it was an opportunity  to try and give something  back to the people in place that have  done so very much  to me i thought this will let me pay  back some of the enormous debts  that i owed to my teachers and the  community that made me into america and  today and you know what just saying  all of you right now sitting in this  room are in exactly the same boat  that i was getting even if you don't  realize it  you have wrapped up this enormous debt  to so many people  just by sitting there and being alive  okay let me try and explain  you owe tax payers for funding public  education  you owe previous generations of business  and their families  whose laundry contributions have paid  for and bought  many of the resources and buildings that  you use today  yes i am still somewhat frustrated that  i didn't get to use the gym  even though i came in six years twice  and you've also earned your parents  for raising you and most of you are like  me  in that you owe them extra because  they didn't just raise you they left  life as they knew it  to come to another country where they  had no money  no family no language all just so that  you could be  born here and receive all the benefits  of being an australian citizen  so we all owe this massive debt to so  many people  and here i was starting out at bruce  thinking yes  i'm going to pay back i'm going to work  hard and be fair  and swear and i did work hard  but the truth of the matter is i never  paid back  any of my debt what a cent  the reason why i never paid in the back  is because the longer i stayed here  the more i benefited from this amazing  place  i benefited from teaching alongside some  of the finest educators in the country  and learning the ropes from them i  benefited from being led by  people who saw it as their mission to  try and train and cultivate younger  teachers like me  and i benefited from being able to  constantly  interact with students like you  who showed me every day without fail  what a privilege it is  to be able to contribute just a little  bit to your journey  toward becoming intelligent creative  adults  you have the passion and the ability to  influence your world to look better  i benefited from all these people the  longer i speak here  the more i went into debt yeah it does  sound a bit depressing doesn't it but it  wasn't  there's a saying i'm sure then you  repair it which goes  you don't pay love back you pay it  forward  you don't pay long back you pay it  forward it took me a long time to  understand this  which is why i'm telling you this today  and i hope that you will understand  earlier than i did  we are all hugely embedded for those who  come for us  those who've led us those who care for  us  you will never ever in a million years  pay that debt back you can only  pay it forward you can only take all the  privileges  and resources and encouragement that  you've received in your lifetime  and pay it forward to the next  generation  you're going to pay it forward to those  people who look to you  as their seniors and their leaders  this is a scary thought their parents  now they'll never be able to repay you  but that's kind of the point  and it's kind of why i became a teacher  so that i could take the way that i was  taught to love  learning and sharing with others  so james ruiz thank you for everything  i thank you for being a place that will  always feel like home even if you do get  a gym  thank you for being people who are  relentless  in becoming the very best students and  citizens and human beings  that you can be and thank you for  teaching me  i thank you for teaching me about myself  this amazing world that we get to live  in and about the importance and the  privilege and the joy  it is to pay it forward

